16) What are trace, video, and screenshot options? How do you use them in CI?
Playwright can record traces, videos, and screenshots to help debug failures. Traces capture screenshots, DOM snapshots, and network activity and can be viewed with the Playwright trace viewer. Videos record the full test run, and screenshots can be taken on failure. In CI, these are usually enabled only for failed tests to save resources, and then uploaded as build artifacts so developers can inspect them when a test fails remotely.

17) How do you run tests in parallel and control workers?
Playwright runs tests in parallel by default using workers, which are separate processes. You can control the number of workers in the config file or via CLI to balance speed and system resources. Tests in different files run in parallel, while tests in the same file run sequentially unless configured otherwise. You can also mark some tests as serial when order matters, though parallel, isolated tests are preferred for scalability.

18) How do you deal with dynamic elements and animations?
Dynamic elements are handled by using stable locators and relying on Playwright’s auto-wait, which waits for elements to become visible and stable. For animations, Playwright waits for elements to stop moving before clicking, but if needed, you can disable animations via CSS injection or wait for specific states using expect assertions. Avoid fixed timeouts and instead wait for meaningful UI states to keep tests reliable.

19) How will you locate the elements in browser one-by-one or in bulk ? 
Ans- I locate elements using robust locators based on priority: unique IDs, data-test attributes, accessible roles, and stable CSS/XPath, following best practices to avoid brittle locators. For one-by-one interaction, I use single element locators like page.locator() in Playwright to perform actions such as click, type, or assert on a specific element with auto-wait and strictness. For bulk operations, like validating lists, tables, menus, or multiple cards, I use a common locator that matches all elements and then iterate using methods like .count(), .nth(), or .allTextContents(). I prefer relative and scoped (filter) locators to narrow context and improve performance.

20) Difference between waitForSelector and relying on auto-wait?
waitForSelector explicitly waits for an element to appear in the DOM before continuing, but it only waits for presence or visibility, not for readiness to interact. Auto-wait is built into actions and assertions and waits for multiple conditions like visibility, enablement, and stability at the moment of action. Relying on auto-wait leads to cleaner and more reliable tests, while waitForSelector should be used sparingly for special cases like waiting for background elements.

21) How do you implement Page Object Model in Playwright?
In Playwright, Page Object Model is implemented by creating classes that represent pages or components, where locators and actions are defined as methods. Each class receives a Page instance in its constructor, and tests use these methods instead of directly interacting with selectors. This improves readability, reduces duplication, and makes maintenance easier when UI changes, while still leveraging Playwright’s powerful locator system.

22) How do you debug a failing test locally and in CI?
Locally, you can run tests in headed mode, debug mode, playwright inspector() and view traces to step through actions. In CI, you rely on collected artifacts like traces, videos, and screenshots to understand what happened. Comparing logs, checking network mocks, and reproducing the failure locally with the same data and config helps close the gap between CI and local environments and fix issues faster.

23) How do you handle JavaScript dialogs (alert/confirm/prompt)?
I register a listener on the dialog event using page.on('dialog'). Once intercepted, I can programmatically accept or dismiss alerts, confirms, or provide input for prompts using dialog.accept() or dialog.dismiss().

24) How do you handle iframes?
To interact with elements inside an iframe, I first obtain the frame reference using page.frame() or frameLocator(). Then I perform actions within the frame context, since DOM inside iframes is isolated from the main page.

25) How do you wait for navigation after a click?
When an action triggers navigation, I explicitly wait for it using page.waitForNavigation() combined with the click inside Promise.all(). This guarantees synchronization between the user action and the resulting page load, preventing flaky tests.

26) How do you take screenshots?
I capture screenshots using page.screenshot(), which allows me to take full-page or viewport images. This is useful for debugging failures and can be integrated into test reports or CI artifacts.”

27) How do you wait for an element to appear?
I wait for an element using page.waitForSelector() or preferably Playwright locators, which have built-in auto-waiting for visibility and attachment to the DOM. This ensures synchronization before performing actions.

28) what is Fixture ? 
A fixture is a reusable setup and teardown mechanism provided by the Playwright Test framework to manage test dependencies such as browser, context, page, test data, or custom utilities. Fixtures enable dependency injection into tests, promote code reuse, and ensure consistent test environments. They can be scoped at test, worker, or suite level and can be extended to create custom fixtures, which improves maintainability and scalability of large automation frameworks.

29) what is Filter on locator
locator.filter() in Playwright is used to refine a locator by applying additional conditions such as visible text, child elements, or other locators. It helps create more readable selectors when multiple elements match the same base locator. By narrowing the search within an existing locator chain, especially in dynamic UIs with repeated components.

30) Command to clear localStorage
In Playwright, localStorage can be cleared by executing JavaScript in the browser context using
await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.clear());
This approach directly interacts with the web application's runtime environment and is useful for resetting client-side state between tests. Clearing localStorage ensures a clean session, avoids data pollution, and helps validate first-time user flows or scenarios dependent on storage-based flags.

31) page.expose function() ?
page.exposeFunction() creates a secure bridge between the browser and Playwright’s Node.js runtime, enabling browser-side JavaScript to call Node.js test logic. This makes it ideal for callbacks, analytics verification, custom mocking, event interception, and advanced test–application integrations where in-app actions need to trigger or validate test-side behavior.

32) Sometimes page load soon and sometimes takes time how can i handle that ?
When a page loads inconsistently, I don’t use hard waits.
I rely on Playwright’s auto-waiting with web-first assertions and explicit waits only for navigation or backend signals.
I wait for what actually indicates readiness — such as a specific UI element, loader disappearance, or API response  which makes tests stable regardless of page speed.” 

33) What are explicit waits in playwright and when to use and example ?
Explicit waits are manual wait APIs used when Playwright’s auto-waiting (locators + assertions) is not sufficient.
Use explicit waits only for navigation, page-level events, or browser events — not for normal UI actions.

34) How can you run specific 10 testcase out of 100 testcase in CI pipeline ?
Ans- In Playwright, I run a specific set  of tests by using tagging, test titles, file paths, or annotations and then passing filters through the CLI. The most common and scalable approach is tagging, where I mark required tests with tags like @smoke or @critical in the test name or test.describe(). Then I execute them using npx playwright test --grep @smoke, which will only run the tests matching that pattern. Alternatively, I can target specific spec files or folders, or use --grep with part of the test title to pick exactly the 10 test cases I need. In a CI pipeline (like Jenkins or GitHub Actions), I parameterize the command so the pipeline runs npx playwright test --grep @smoke or a custom tag, enabling dynamic selection without changing code. This approach ensures fast feedback by running only critical tests on each build,

1️⃣ page.waitForSelector()
Waits for an element to appear in the DOM and reach a given state (attached, visible, hidden, detached).
EX - await page.waitForSelector('#toast', { state: 'visible' });

✅ When to use
Element appears dynamically outside locator usage
Legacy code / debugging
Waiting before non-locator logic
❌ Avoid when
You can use locator.click(), locator.fill()
You can assert using expect(locator)
👉 Preferred alternative
await expect(page.locator('#toast')).toBeVisible();

2️⃣ page.waitForLoadState()
Waits for page loading lifecycle events.

Load states
load → full page load event fired
domcontentloaded → DOM ready
networkidle → no network requests for ~500ms

EX - await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');

✅ When to use
After page.goto()
After opening a new tab/window
Pages heavily dependent on API calls
❌ Avoid
Waiting for specific UI elements
Replacing assertions
🔴 Common mistake
await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle'); // ❌ flaky for SPAs

3️⃣ page.waitForNavigation()
Waits for a full page navigation (URL + document reload).

EX - 
await Promise.all([
  page.waitForNavigation(),
  page.click('a[href="/dashboard"]'),
]);

✅ When to use
Clicking links that cause full page reload
Form submissions with navigation
❌ Avoid
Single-Page Applications (React, Angular, Vue)
Client-side routing
👉 SPA alternative
await page.waitForURL('**/dashboard');

4️⃣ page.waitForEvent()
Waits for browser-level events.
EX - const newPage = await page.waitForEvent('popup');

✅ Use cases
New tab / popup
File download
Dialogs

EX 
const [download] = await Promise.all([
  page.waitForEvent('download'),
  page.click('#download'), 
]);

5️⃣ page.waitForURL()
Waits until the current URL matches a pattern.
EX- await page.waitForURL('**/checkout');

✅ Best for
SPA routing
Redirects without reload
OAuth flows

